 ## Overview
-Half-Angle Slice Rendering is a technique for computing volumetric shadows and local illumination in participating media, specifically designed for particle systems and voxel-based atmospheric data [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently accumulating shadowing information while rendering volume slices by projecting the volume onto a single 2D shadow buffer [S2, unofficial]. Unlike methods that require complex 3D texture lookups or separate light-space rendering passes, this method calculates a "half-angle" vector between the view and light directions, allowing the same set of slices to be composited into both an eye buffer (for final image accumulation) and a light buffer (for shadow attenuation) [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Volumetric Soft Shadowing](4507-volumetric-soft-shadowing.md) by relying on discrete slicing and 2D buffer accumulation rather than continuous ray marching or kernel-based soft shadow approximations.
